#block(inset: (x: 0.32in))[
 #set text(size: 11pt)
 #set par(first-line-indent: 0pt)
 Embedded microfluidic cooling is a promising technology for dissipating
the intense heat generated by high-performance electronic chips.
However, the design and optimization of such coolers impose prohibitive
computational costs when using conventional numerical solvers. Here, we
present a parameterized physics-informed neural network (PINN) as a deep
learning framework for rapidly and accurately predicting the
thermohydraulic performance of embedded manifold microchannels. By
incorporating four geometric dimensions along with three spatial
coordinates as the network inputs, the proposed model enables
multiphysics prediction across a continuous design space. Remarkably,
our predicted velocity and temperature fields differ from finite-element
simulations by relative errors of only 8.1% and 5.6%, respectively,
while reducing the computational time and cost by over four orders of
magnitude. Key to this framework is a composite strategy combining
geometric ansatz embedding, the SOAP optimizer, and a
thermal-conductivity-based curriculum learning scheme, which helps
overcome the convergence challenges inherent in training PINNs for
complex three-dimensional flows. Notably, by leveraging a highly
optimized JAX backend, the network achieves convergence on a single
consumer-grade GPU in less than four hours. Our approach offers a
physics-consistent, real-time surrogate model that can substantially
accelerate innovations in advanced microfluidic cooling of future
electronics.
]

= Introduction
<sec1>
The continuous escalation of power density in integrated circuits
necessitates more advanced and efficient thermal dissipation strategies
@fabbri_state_2007@tuckerman_high-performance_1981. For instance, in
high-performance computing, microprocessors routinely generate heat
fluxes that exceed the capacity of conventional air cooling
@zhang_survey_2021. Moreover, radio-frequency and power electronics
based on wide-bandgap semiconductors such as silicon carbide and gallium
nitride operate at much higher power densities @bar-cohen_embedded_2019.
Without adequate cooling, elevated junction temperatures degrade device
performance, reduce reliability, and shorten operational lifespan
@bar-cohen_icecool_2021. Consequently, there has been a marked shift
toward advanced liquid cooling research to address these escalating
thermal demands @fabbri_state_2007@van_erp_co-designing_2020.

Among diverse approaches, embedded microfluidic cooling has emerged as a
promising solution for dissipating ultrahigh heat fluxes
@escher_experimental_2010@van_erp_co-designing_2020@wu_jet-enhanced_2025.
Unlike conventional cold plates which are mounted on the chip surface
via a layer of thermal interfacial material, embedded cooling leverages
microfluidic structures that are etched directly into the backside of a
chip. This approach brings the coolant as close as possible to the
hotspots, which fundamentally reduces the thermal resistance and
therefore enables unprecedented cooling performance. In particular,
manifold microchannel configurations offer unique advantages, which help
maintain a low pressure drop while substantially enhancing heat transfer
@lin_single-phase_2021. Remarkably, recent studies have numerically
predicted and experimentally demonstrated that embedded manifold
microchannels (EMMC) with single-phase water can dissipate heat fluxes
exceeding 3000~W~cm#super[−2] over a heated area of 9~mm#super[2]
@wu_jet_2023@wu_efficient_2024@wu_jet-enhanced_2025. In addition,
embedded cooling with a dielectric fluid has also achieved record-high
heat fluxes up to 1570~W~cm#super[−2] @xiao_horizontal-up_2026. Of key
importance is the geometric parameters of these structures which
strongly impact the thermal and hydraulic performance
@jiang_synergistic_2026@jiang_embedded_2026@li_biomimetic_2026.
Therefore, tailored design and optimization for a specific chip becomes
necessary when moving towards real-world applications. However,
parametric optimization of EMMC requires a great number of high-fidelity
simulations of three-dimensional (3D) conjugate heat transfer, which is
expensive especially when using conventional approaches such as the
finite-element method (FEM). When exploring a design space with multiple
geometric parameters, the total computational cost becomes prohibitive
@jiang_synergistic_2026@yang_parameterized_2025.

To accelerate thermohydraulic simulation and reduce the cost of
optimization, machine learning (ML) has emerged as a promising
framework. Recently, data-driven ML approaches have been widely employed
in thermal science, particularly for the prediction of thermophysical
properties and the design of thermal functional materials and devices
@yu_general_2023@hu_machine-learning-optimized_2020@hu_machine_2020@yu_ultrahigh_2024@chen_strong_2025@zhou_insight_2025@wu_correcting_2024.
Furthermore, automated data analysis for laser pump-probe thermal
measurement has been demonstrated @yang_vibe-fdtr_2026. Complementary to
various data-driven methods, physics-informed neural networks (PINNs)
@raissi_physics-informed_2019 stand as an appealing alternative for fluid
flow and heat transfer modeling. PINNs incorporate the governing partial
differential equations (PDEs) directly into the loss function, enabling
the network to maintain physical consistency and learn solutions with
little or even no labeled training data
@raissi_physics-informed_2019@cuomo_scientific_2022. Given their diverse
advantages, PINNs have been applied to various problems in fluid
mechanics and heat transfer
@raissi_hidden_2020@hennigh_nvidia_2021@cuomo_scientific_2022@zhang_physics-informed_2024@yang_parameterized_2025@wang_simulating_2025.
Notably, Hennigh et al. @hennigh_nvidia_2021 pioneered parameterized
PINN simulations for heat sinks using the NVIDIA SimNet™ framework
(currently known as PhysicsNeMo Sym @noauthor_physicsnemo_nodate). Zhang
et al. utilized PINNs to simulate conjugate heat transfer in manifold
microchannel heat sinks, focusing on modeling for a fixed geometry
@zhang_physics-informed_2024. Yang et al. proposed a parameterized PINN
for the thermal design of a power module based on a layer of
millimeter-scale pin-fins @yang_parameterized_2025. Despite such
progress, it remains a great challenge to apply PINNs to complex 3D
conjugate heat transfer with parameterized geometries. First, the
computational cost of training 3D PINNs remains high
@zhang_physics-informed_2024@hennigh_nvidia_2021. Further, the training
of PINNs for convection-dominated heat transfer faces a variety of
optimization difficulties
@wang_experts_2023@krishnapriyan_characterizing_2021. Finally,
parameterized geometries introduce additional complexity that as design
parameters change, the spatial relationship between sampling points and
domain boundaries varies, making it difficult for networks to
distinguish boundaries from interior regions.

Here, we present a parameterized PINN for the 3D simulation of fluid
flow and heat transfer in embedded jet-enhanced manifold microchannels,
overcoming the aforementioned challenges through several methodological
contributions. First, a low cost geometric ansatz embedding strategy
encodes the relative spatial position of each point within the
parameterized geometry, providing explicit geometric information
essential for convergence. Second, a curriculum learning scheme
@bengio_curriculum_2009 transitions training from diffusion-dominated to
convection-dominated regimes by gradually adjusting the effective
Prandtl number and the fluid--solid conductivity ratio. This mitigates
instabilities common in PINN training for high-Prandtl-number flows.
Third, the SOAP optimizer @vyas_soap_2025 combined with gradient
normalization @wang_experts_2023 enables stable convergence in this
multiphysics problem. Fourth, the implementation uses a framework based
on JAX @bradbury_jax_2018@deepmind_deepmind_2020 with forward-mode
automatic differentiation @baydin_automatic_2018@li_computational_2024,
achieving efficient training on a single consumer-grade GPU in only four
hours. Our network accepts the three spatial coordinates along with four
geometric parameters as inputs, including microchannel height, width,
and jet inlet/outlet widths. Once trained, the model predicts the
coupled flow and temperature fields for any parameter combination in a
continuous design space within milliseconds, enabling over four orders
of magnitude reduction in per-design computational time and cost
relative to conventional finite-element simulations on a 56-core
workstation. Validation against commercial FEM software shows relative
errors of 8.1% and 5.6% for the velocity and temperature fields,
respectively. The one-time training cost is comparable in wall-clock
time to only 108 single-design FEM simulations, a level that is readily
offset in parameter sweeps and optimization studies involving larger
numbers of design evaluations. This physics-consistent surrogate model
can therefore substantially accelerate the design and optimization of
embedded microfluidic coolers.

= Methods
<sec2>
== Physical model and geometric parameterization
<physical-model-and-geometric-parameterization>
In this study, we employ an embedded jet-enhanced manifold microchannel
cooler as a representative complex three-dimensional conjugate
heat-transfer system consisting of solid and fluid regions. As shown in
@fig:geometry\(a)-(c), the computational domain is symmetry-reduced
from the entire cooler to a single microchannel unit, with symmetry
boundary conditions imposed on the corresponding planes to minimize
cost. The solid domain is the substrate that conducts heat from the
active device layer to the coolant, while the fluid domain represents
the coolant passages. Without loss of generality, we consider here a
silicon substrate in conjunction with water as the coolant.

To define the parameterized design space, four key geometric dimensions
are incorporated including the jet inlet width
$\( W_(upright(i n)) in \[ 40 \, 50 \] med upright("μ") upright("m") \)$,
the jet outlet width
$\( W_(upright(o u t)) in \[ 90 \, 110 \] med upright("μ") upright("m") \)$,
the microchannel width
$\( W_(upright(m c)) in \[ 45 \, 55 \] med upright("μ") upright("m") \)$,
and the microchannel height
$\( H_(upright(m c)) in \[ 140 \, 160 \] med upright("μ") upright("m") \)$.
These parameters are selected based on prior design studies in which
they were found to have a strong influence on the thermohydraulic
performance of the device @jiang_synergistic_2026. Their ranges are
chosen to remain compatible with practical manufacturing constraints
while covering a sufficiently large design space.

Steady-state heat transfer in the EMMC structure is governed by the
incompressible Navier--Stokes and energy equations in the fluid domain,
and by the heat conduction equation in the solid domain. The 3D space is
captured by the Cartesian coordinate
$upright(bold(r)) = \[ x \, y \, z \]$. In the fluid domain,
conservation of mass requires $ nabla dot.op upright(bold(u)) = 0 \, $
where $upright(bold(u)) = \( u \, v \, w \)$ denotes the velocity
vector. Conservation of momentum is expressed as
$ \( upright(bold(u)) dot.op nabla \) upright(bold(u)) = - 1 / rho nabla p + nu nabla^2 upright(bold(u)) \, $
where $p$ is the pressure, $rho$ is the fluid density, and $nu$ is the
kinematic viscosity. The fluid energy equation is
$ \( upright(bold(u)) dot.op nabla \) T_(upright(f)) = alpha_(upright(f)) nabla^2 T_(upright(f)) \, $
where $T_(upright(f))$ is the fluid temperature and $alpha_(upright(f))$
is the thermal diffusivity of the fluid. In the solid domain, heat
conduction is governed by $ nabla^2 T_(upright(s)) = 0 \, $ where
$T_(upright(s))$ is the solid temperature.

At the fluid-solid interface, temperature heat-flux continuity are
imposed. Other conditions include the inlet and outlet boundaries in the
fluid domain, the isothermal base and adiabatic top boundary in the
solid domain, and also the symmetry planes. All thermophysical
properties are treated as constants for simplicity, therefore the
momentum equations are independent of temperature, while the energy
equation depends on the velocity field.

The initial parameterized input vector is defined as
$ upright(bold(x))_(upright(i n p u t)) = \[ x \, y \, z \, W_(upright(i n)) \, W_(upright(o u t)) \, W_(upright(m c)) \, H_(upright(m c)) \] . $
This formulation shifts the learning target from a single fixed geometry
to a family of solutions parameterized by geometry.

== Parameterized PINN framework
<parameterized-pinn-framework>
A parameterized physics-informed neural network (PINN) is used to learn
the mapping from spatial coordinates and geometric parameters to the
corresponding physical fields. Specifically, a neural network
$N N_theta$ parameterized by weights and biases $theta$ is constructed
to approximate the state vector as
$ \[ u \, v \, w \, p \, T_(upright(f)) \, T_(upright(s)) \] = N N_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g)) \) \, $
where $upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g))$ denotes the augmented network
input. The network is trained by minimizing a composite loss that
includes the governing-equation residuals and the boundary-condition
terms.

The network backbone is based on PirateNet @wang_piratenets_2024. To
improve the representation of multiscale features, the input is first
mapped into a higher-dimensional space using Fourier feature embeddings
@rahaman_spectral_2019,
$ gamma \( upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g)) \) = [cos (2 pi upright(bold(W))_(upright(e m b)) upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g))) \, sin (2 pi upright(bold(W))_(upright(e m b)) upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g)))] \, $
where $upright(bold(W))_(upright(e m b)) in bb(R)^(d times m)$ is a
learnable weight matrix and $m$ is the embedding dimension, set to 256
in this study. The final network consists of four PirateNet residual
blocks, each with 256 hidden units. Random weight factorization is
additionally used to facilitate optimization @wang_random_2022.

To better represent the parameterized geometry, the initial input
$upright(bold(x))_(upright(i n p u t))$ is further augmented with
geometric ansatz embeddings. Variations in the geometric parameters can
change both the domain to which a point belongs and its relative
position with respect to nearby boundaries. To encode this information
explicitly, normalized local coordinates are defined for each coordinate
direction within the distinct domains:
$ upright(bold(g))_i = frac(upright(bold(r))_i - upright(bold(r))_(i \, min) \( upright(bold(p)) \), upright(bold(r))_(i \, max) \( upright(bold(p)) \) - upright(bold(r))_(i \, min) \( upright(bold(p)) \)) \, $
where
$upright(bold(p)) = \[ W_(upright(i n)) \, W_(upright(o u t)) \, W_(upright(m c)) \, H_(upright(m c)) \]$
is the current vector of geometric parameters and
$upright(bold(r)) = \[ x \, y \, z \]$ is the spatial coordinate. These
local coordinates are then transformed by a smooth embedding function.
In this work, a sigmoid function $sigma_(upright(s))$ is adopted,
$ sigma_s \( x \) = frac(1, 1 + e^(- s x)) \, $ and the mapped ansatz
feature is defined as
$ tilde(upright(bold(g)))_i = sigma_s (frac(upright(bold(g))_i - 0.5, delta)) \, $
where $delta$ is a scaling parameter controlling the sharpness of the
transition. The augmented network input is then written as
$ upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g)) = \[ upright(bold(x))_(upright(n o r m)) \, tilde(upright(bold(g))) \] \, $
where $upright(bold(x))_(upright(n o r m))$ comprises seven normalized
spatial coordinates and geometric parameters, and
$tilde(upright(bold(g)))$ denotes eight mapped local geometric features,
which together constitute a fifteen-dimensional network input in the
present implementation, as illustrated in @fig:ansatz\(b).

Training points are generated on the fly in JAX @bradbury_jax_2018
rather than pre-generated externally. The three-dimensional fluid and
solid domains are algebraically decomposed into parameterized bounding
boxes for interior regions and boundary planes for surface constraints.
Interior points are sampled uniformly over the composite multi-block
regions corresponding to the jet inlet, microchannels, and jet outlet,
whereas boundary points are sampled directly on the corresponding
parameterized surfaces. A region-aware importance sampling strategy is
further used so that more points are placed near the fluid-solid walls,
narrow microchannel gaps, and inlet/outlet boundaries, where velocity
and temperature gradients are strongest. Because the sampling is
performed directly on a GPU, repeated CPU-to-GPU data transfer is
avoided during training, thereby substantially improving performance.

== Loss formulation
<loss-formulation>
To improve numerical conditioning, all governing equations and boundary
conditions are cast into a nondimensional form. The characteristic
scales used in this work are
$L_(upright(r e f)) = 100 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ for the length,
$U_(upright(r e f)) = 1 med upright("m") upright("/") upright("s")$ for
the velocity, $P_(upright(r e f)) = rho U_(upright(r e f))^2$ for the
pressure, and $T_(upright(r e f)) = 50 med upright("K")$ for the
temperature. The corresponding nondimensional variables are defined as
$ upright(bold(x))^(*) = upright(bold(x)) / L_(upright(r e f)) \, #h(2em) upright(bold(u))^(*) = upright(bold(u)) / U_(upright(r e f)) \, #h(2em) p^(*) = frac(p, rho U_(upright(r e f))^2) \, #h(2em) T^(*) = frac(Delta T, T_(upright(r e f))) . $

The fluid continuity and momentum equations become
$ nabla^(*) dot.op upright(bold(u))^(*) = 0 \, $
$ \( upright(bold(u))^(*) dot.op nabla^(*) \) upright(bold(u))^(*) = - nabla^(*) p^(*) + frac(1, R e) nabla^(* 2) upright(bold(u))^(*) \, $
while the energy equations in the fluid and solid domains are written
respectively as
$ \( upright(bold(u))^(*) dot.op nabla^(*) \) T_f^(*) = frac(1, R e dot.op P r) nabla^(* 2) T_f^(*) \, $<eq:energy_f>
$ nabla^(* 2) T_s^(*) = 0 . $ Here,
$ R e = frac(U_(upright(r e f)) L_(upright(r e f)), nu) \, #h(2em) P r = nu / alpha_f \, $
denote the Reynolds number and Prandtl number, respectively. At the
fluid--solid interface, continuity of heat flux gives
$ frac(partial T_f^(*), partial n^(*)) = k^(*) frac(partial T_s^(*), partial n^(*)) \, $
where $ k^(*) = k_s / k_f $ denotes the thermal conductivity ratio.

To keep the network outputs within prescribed ranges, a linear output
scaling is appended to the final layer:
$ \[ u^(*) \, v^(*) \, w^(*) \, p^(*) \, T_f^(*) \, T_s^(*) \] = frac(N N_theta \( upright(bold(x))_(upright(a u g)) \) + 1, 2) dot.op \( upright(bold(y))_max - upright(bold(y))_min \) + upright(bold(y))_min \, $
where $upright(bold(y))_min$ and $upright(bold(y))_max$ are empirical
lower and upper bounds for the output variables.

The interior PDE losses are defined as the mean-squared residuals of the
governing equations evaluated at dynamically sampled points. For the
fluid domain, the continuity and momentum residuals are
$ cal(L)_(upright(c o n t)) = 1 / N_f sum_(i = 1)^(N_f) (nabla^(*) dot.op upright(bold(u))^(*))^2 \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(m o m)) = 1 / N_f sum_(i = 1)^(N_f) ∥\( upright(bold(u))^(*) dot.op nabla^(*) \) upright(bold(u))^(*) + nabla^(*) p^(*) - frac(1, R e) nabla^(* 2) upright(bold(u))^(*)∥^2 . $

The energy residuals in the fluid and solid domains are
$ cal(L)_(upright(e n) \, f) = 1 / N_f sum_(i = 1)^(N_f) [\( upright(bold(u))^(*) dot.op nabla^(*) \) T_f^(*) - frac(1, R e dot.op P r) nabla^(* 2) T_f^(*)]^2 \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(e n) \, s) = 1 / N_s sum_(i = 1)^(N_s) (nabla^(* 2) T_s^(*))^2 . $

Boundary and interface conditions are enforced through additional loss
terms. For the inlet boundary, the hydraulic and thermal constraints are
defined separately because the thermal part is introduced progressively
during training:
$ cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, s c a l a r)) = 1 / N_(upright(i n) \, f) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(i n) \, f)) [\( p^(*) - p_(upright(i n))^(*) \)^2 + \( u^(*) \)^2 + \( v^(*) \)^2] \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, f l u x)) = 1 / N_(upright(i n) \, f) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(i n) \, f)) (frac(1, R e dot.op P r) frac(partial T_f^(*), partial n^(*)) - w^(*) T_f^(*))^2 \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, t e m p)) = 1 / N_(upright(i n) \, f) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(i n) \, f)) (T_f^(*))^2 + 1 / N_(upright(i n) \, s) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(i n) \, s)) (T_s^(*) - T_(upright(a u x))^(*))^2 \, $
where $T_(upright(a u x))^(*) = 0.8$ is an auxiliary non-dimensional
temperature target used only during the early stage of training. The
value is chosen empirically as a numerically convenient thermal anchor
rather than a physically exact constraint. Since the weight of this
auxiliary term decays to zero during training, it does not affect the
final boundary condition enforced by the model.

At the outlet boundary,
$ cal(L)_(upright(o u t l e t)) = 1 / N_(upright(o u t)) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(o u t))) [\( p^(*) \)^2 + \( u^(*) \)^2 + \( v^(*) \)^2 + (frac(partial T_f^(*), partial n^(*)))^2] . $

For the heated base of the solid domain,
$ cal(L)_(upright(b a s e) \, s) = 1 / N_(upright(b a s e) \, s) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(b a s e) \, s)) (T_s^(*) - T_(upright(b a s e))^(*))^2 . $

For the solid top boundary,
$ cal(L)_(upright(t o p)) = 1 / N_(upright(t o p)) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(t o p))) (frac(partial T_s^(*), partial n^(*)))^2 . $<eq:top_loss>

At the fluid-solid interface, no-slip, temperature continuity, and
heat-flux continuity are imposed through
$ cal(L)_(upright(n o s l i p)) = 1 / N_w sum_(i = 1)^(N_w) parallel upright(bold(u))^(*) parallel^2 \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(t e m p \, c o n t)) = 1 / N_w sum_(i = 1)^(N_w) (T_f^(*) - T_s^(*))^2 \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(f l u x \, c o n t)) = 1 / N_w sum_(i = 1)^(N_w) (frac(partial T_f^(*), partial n^(*)) - k^(*) frac(partial T_s^(*), partial n^(*)))^2 . $

For the symmetry planes, the velocity and scalar losses are defined as
$ cal(L)_(upright(s y m) \, u) = 1 / N_(upright(s y m) \, f) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(s y m) \, f)) [\( upright(bold(u))^(*) dot.op upright(bold(n)) \)^2 + ∥\( upright(bold(I)) - upright(bold(n)) upright(bold(n))^T \) frac(partial upright(bold(u))^(*), partial n^(*))∥^2] \, $
$ cal(L)_(upright(s y m) \, phi.alt) = 1 / N_(upright(s y m)) sum_(i = 1)^(N_(upright(s y m))) (frac(partial phi.alt, partial n^(*)))^2 \, #h(2em) phi.alt in { p^(*) \, T_f^(*) \, T_s^(*) } . $

== Training strategy and implementation
<sec2_4>
Curriculum learning is employed to enhance the robustness of PINN
training for the present conjugate heat-transfer problem. Such a
strategy is necessary because the target regime is convection dominated
and involves a strong thermal-conductivity contrast across the
fluid-solid interface, which significantly increases the training
difficulty. To this end, a unified continuation factor is introduced:
$ alpha \( t \) = min (t / t_(upright(w a r m u p)) \, 1) . $ Here,
$t_(upright(w a r m u p)) = 25 \, 000$, which is one eighth of the total
200,000 training steps. This factor is used consistently to control the
thermal curriculum and activation of the heated-base thermal constraint.

The effective Prandtl number used during training is scheduled as
$ P r_(upright(e f f)) \( t \) = P r_min + (P r_(upright(t a r g e t)) - P r_min) alpha \( t \) \, $
where $P r_min = 10^(- 6)$ and $P r_(upright(t a r g e t)) = 6.977$. At
the early stage of training, the small effective Prandtl number
suppresses the stiffness associated with convection-dominated thermal
transport and allows the network to first learn an easier
diffusion-dominated temperature field.

A similar continuation is applied to the conductivity ratio appearing in
the fluid-solid interface condition:
$ k_(upright(e f f))^(*) \( t \) = 1 + (k_(upright(t a r g e t))^(*) - 1) alpha \( t \) \, $
where $k_(upright(t a r g e t))^(*) = 250$. Starting from matched
conductivities simplifies the conjugate heat-transfer coupling at the
interface and gradually restores the target physics as training
proceeds.

In addition to parameter continuation, an auxiliary thermal boundary
loss is introduced to stabilize the early stage of optimization. The
auxiliary terms at the inlet and top surfaces share a common fading
schedule $beta \( t \) = min \( t \/ 100 \, 000 \, 1 \)$, which extends
beyond the main curriculum. The inlet loss is assembled as
$ cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t)) \( t \) = cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, s c a l a r)) + beta \( t \) cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, f l u x)) + [1 - beta \( t \)] cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, t e m p)) \, $
so that training begins with a simpler temperature-value constraint and
gradually transitions to the inflow condition. Because the weight of
$cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, t e m p))$ decays to zero, this auxiliary
term only guides early optimization and does not alter the final
physics.

Similarly, the heated-base loss is defined as
$ cal(L)_(upright(b a s e)) \( t \) = cal(L)_(upright(b a s e) \, s) + alpha \( t \) cal(L)_(upright(b a s e) \, f) . $
This gradual activation improves the thermal coupling between the heated
solid base and the fluid region and reduces optimization drift in the
coupled thermal problem.

The top-surface loss is likewise augmented with an auxiliary temperature
term during the early stage of training,
$ cal(L)_(upright(t o p)) \( t \) = cal(L)_(upright(t o p))^(upright(a d i a)) + [1 - beta \( t \)] cal(L)_(upright(t o p))^(upright(a u x)) \, $
which prevents the solid temperature field from collapsing into a
trivial flat solution.

During training, the constant $P r$ in the fluid-energy residual and the
inlet loss is replaced by $P r_(upright(e f f)) \( t \)$, and the
conductivity ratio $k^(*)$ in the interface loss is replaced by
$k_(upright(e f f))^(*) \( t \)$.

The total loss is therefore time dependent and can be written as
$ cal(L)_(upright(t o t a l)) \( t \) = sum_(k = 1)^K w_k^(\( t \)) cal(L)_k \( t \) \, $
where the loss terms include the PDE residuals, the boundary and
interface constraints, and the time-dependent inlet, base, and top
losses defined above. Because the different loss components can differ
substantially in magnitude during training, dynamic gradient
normalization is used to balance them @wang_experts_2023.

Optimization is performed using the SOAP optimizer in its schedule-free
variant @defazio_road_2024@vyas_soap_2025@wang_gradient_2025. SOAP
combines matrix preconditioning with Adam-style momentum and bias
correction, and has been found to improve the robustness of PINN
training for stiff multiphysics problems. In the present implementation,
the base learning rate is set to $eta = 10^(- 3)$. A linear warm-up is
applied over the first 5,000 steps, after which the learning rate decays
exponentially by a factor of 0.97 every 2,000 steps during the main
stage of training.

The entire framework is implemented in JAX to leverage XLA compilation
@bradbury_jax_2018 and GPU execution. A key computational issue in PINNs
is the repeated evaluation of first- and second-order spatial
derivatives required by the PDE residuals. In the present problem, the
network maps a low-dimensional input vector, consisting of spatial
coordinates and geometric parameters, to multiple physical fields, which
results in a tall Jacobian with respect to the inputs. For this reason,
forward-mode automatic differentiation is used for coordinate
derivatives, together with reverse-mode backpropagation for parameter
updates. This choice is well suited to the present setting and reduces
the cost of evaluating the spatial derivatives required by the PDE
residuals. The complete training loop, including point sampling, loss
evaluation, and optimizer updates, is compiled with just-in-time
compiler of JAX to reduce Python overhead. Under the baseline setting,
training runs for 200,000 iterations and requires less than four hours
on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090D GPU, with a peak memory usage of
approximately 20~GB.

= Results and discussion
<sec3>
== Model validation against CFD
<sec3_1>
To assess the predictive accuracy of the proposed PINN model over the
entire design space, FEM references are generated using COMSOL
Multiphysics 6.3. Because all thermophysical properties were treated as
constants, the present flow and heat-transfer problem is effectively
one-way coupled: the velocity and pressure fields affect the temperature
field, whereas temperature does not feed back into the momentum
equation. The references therefore employ a one-way coupled solution
strategy, which is fully consistent with the governing physics and
avoids unnecessary computational overhead. In this way, the FEM baseline
is not artificially disadvantaged in the comparison. Following the
grid-independence assessment reported in our previous work on a similar
microchannel configuration @wu_jet_2023, an extra-fine mesh with
approximately $2.3 times 10^6$ elements is adopted for all FEM cases.

A full-factorial scan is then performed over the parameter space defined
in Section~@sec2. Five values are taken for each of the four geometric
parameters, yielding a total of $5^4 = 625$ FEM simulations. For each
parameter combination, 10,000 random evaluation points are sampled in
the relevant computational domains, and the reference fields are
interpolated onto these locations. In total, this produces
$6.25 times 10^6$ point-wise comparisons for evaluation of the trained
PINN, which is considerably stricter than comparing only global
quantities such as pressure drop or peak temperature.

The relative mean squared errors (MSEs) are defined as:
$ upright("Error")_u = frac(1 / N sum_(i = 1)^N parallel upright(bold(u))_(upright(p r e d) \, i) - upright(bold(u))_(upright(r e f) \, i) parallel_2^2, 1 / N sum_(i = 1)^N parallel upright(bold(u))_(upright(r e f) \, i) parallel_2^2 + epsilon.alt) times 100 % , $ <eq:err_u>

$ upright("Error")_T = frac(1 / N sum_(i = 1)^N \( T_(upright(p r e d) \, i) - T_(upright(r e f) \, i) \)^2, 1 / N sum_(i = 1)^N T_(upright(r e f) \, i)^2 + epsilon.alt) times 100 % , $ <eq:err_T>
where $N$ is the number of evaluation points for the corresponding field
and $epsilon.alt = 10^(- 8)$ is introduced to avoid division by zero.
Accordingly, the relative $L_2$ error is defined as the square root of
the relative MSE. The quantitative errors of the full PINN model are
summarized in the first row of @tab:validation_ablation. The
trained PINN achieves relative MSEs of 0.66% and 0.31% for the velocity
and temperature fields, respectively, corresponding to relative $L_2$
errors (i.e., the square root of the relative MSEs) of 8.1% and 5.6%.

@fig:validation_fields further compares the FEM and PINN
predictions for a representative geometry with
$W_(upright(i n)) = 45 med upright("μ") upright("m")$,
$W_(upright(o u t)) = 100 med upright("μ") upright("m")$,
$W_(upright(m c)) = 50 med upright("μ") upright("m")$, and
$H_(upright(m c)) = 150 med upright("μ") upright("m")$. These values lie
in the center of the parameter space. Overall, the PINN reproduces the
main three-dimensional thermohydraulic features with good point-wise
agreement. In the pressure field, it captures both the strong pressure
gradient induced by the inlet jet and the local pressure rise in the
bottom impingement region where the flow turns into the microchannel
section. The predicted fluid-temperature field also matches the FEM
reference closely, with the largest discrepancies localized near the
outlet. In the solid domain, the model recovers both the dominant
through-thickness temperature gradient and the overall heat-spreading
pattern in the silicon substrate. Likewise, the velocity-magnitude field
is well reproduced, including the localized high-speed regions
associated with the inlet jet and the constricted outlet passage. The
error maps indicate that the largest pressure discrepancies are mainly
concentrated in the microchannel region, whereas the agreement near the
inlet and outlet is generally better. Notably, even in the jet inlet
region, where the field gradients are strongest, the prediction error
remains relatively low. These results confirm that the proposed PINN
captures the key local features of the coupled flow and heat transfer
fields with good fidelity across regions of both moderate and strong
spatial variations.

Beyond agreement for a single representative case, @fig:streamlines
highlights the parametric nature of the learned solution by showing the
velocity magnitude and streamlines at the
$y = 75 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ cross-section for different
combinations of $H_(upright(m c))$ and $W_(upright(i n))$. As the
geometry changes, the predicted flow field deforms smoothly with the
domain, while remaining fully consistent with the evolving boundaries.
In particular, the no-slip walls and channel interfaces are respected
across all four configurations, which reflects the effectiveness of the
geometric ansatz in embedding geometric constraints into the network
input. The influence of the geometric parameters is also physically
meaningful. Under the fixed-pressure boundary condition used here,
increasing the jet inlet width from
$W_(upright(i n)) = 40 med upright("μ") upright("m")$ to 50~μm reduces
the hydraulic resistance and leads to a higher overall flow rate. This
trend is clearly captured by the PINN through the expansion of the
high-velocity regions, especially the bright yellow acceleration zone
near the outlet side. At the same time, the main flow structures are
preserved across the parameter variations, including the large
recirculating vortex in the microchannel, the low-velocity impingement
and turning region near the bottom wall, and the accelerated flow near
the jet inlet and outlet. These structures shift and rescale in a
physically plausible manner as $H_(upright(m c))$ and $W_(upright(i n))$
vary, indicating that the model has learned not merely isolated
solutions at discrete geometries, but a continuous parameterized mapping
between geometry and flow field. It is also worth emphasizing that the
trained network accepts the geometric parameters as continuous inputs.
Therefore, although validation is carried out on the 625 reference
cases, the resulting surrogate is not restricted to these discrete
points and can be queried at unseen parameter combinations within the
trained design space.

== Ablation studies
<sec3_2>
The remaining rows of @tab:validation_ablation compare the full
model with several ablated variants. Here, "Auxiliary loss" denotes the
early-stage thermal boundary continuation introduced in Section~@sec2_4,
where simplified inlet, base, and top-surface temperature constraints
are gradually replaced by the full thermal boundary conditions. Overall,
removing any one of the proposed components increases the temperature
error, indicating that the final performance arises from the combined
contributions of geometry-aware embedding, stable optimization, and
staged training.

Several important observations can be drawn from
@fig:ablation_loss. First, the geometric ansatz is indispensable
for parameterized geometries. Once it is removed, the training loss
rapidly stagnates at approximately $10^1$ and no meaningful convergence
is achieved. This behavior is expected: as the geometric parameters
vary, the same absolute coordinate may correspond to very different
relative positions with respect to the fluid-solid interface and the
external boundaries. Without explicit geometric embedding, the network
cannot reliably distinguish boundary regions from the interior, and
therefore fails to learn the correct solution family.

Second, the schedule-free strategy plays a critical role in optimization
stability. Replacing it with a conventional scheduled optimization
strategy (denoted as "w/o Schedule-free" in the ablation study) results
in convergence to a significantly higher loss plateau, preventing the
model from reaching the performance of the full configuration. Likewise,
removing the auxiliary loss---that is, discarding the transitional terms
$cal(L)_(upright(i n l e t \, t e m p))$ and
$cal(L)_(upright(b a s e) \, f)$, together with the top-surface
temperature term, and imposing the target thermal boundary formulation
from the beginning of training---introduces clear late-stage
instability. Although the loss initially decreases, it starts to rise
again after approximately $4 times 10^4$ training steps, suggesting that
the auxiliary continuation is important for suppressing optimization
drift in this coupled multiphysics problem.

The role of curriculum learning is further clarified by the field
comparisons in @fig:ablation_fields. As indicated by the green
curve in @fig:ablation_loss, the model trained without curriculum
learning converges more rapidly and achieves the lowest training loss
among all configurations. However, its relative MSE for temperature
increases from 0.31% to 0.39%. Thus, curriculum learning trades a modest
amount of flow-field accuracy for improved temperature fidelity, which
is the primary quantity of interest. This result also highlights that,
for the present convection-dominated thermohydraulic problem, a lower
aggregated PINN loss does not necessarily imply a more accurate physical
solution.

The effect of SOAP is also instructive. The run without SOAP reaches a
final training loss comparable to that of the full model
(@fig:ablation_loss), yet its velocity and temperature errors are
markedly larger: removing SOAP increases the relative MSE of velocity
from 0.66% to 1.41% and the temperature MSE from 0.31% to 1.49%, which
again underlines that the aggregated training loss alone is not a
reliable indicator of field accuracy in the present problem. The
pressure field is not emphasized here, since it is directly constrained
by the boundary conditions and is therefore less representative of the
model's ability to capture the more challenging transport dynamics. By
contrast, velocity and especially temperature provide a more meaningful
assessment for the present thermohydraulic problem.

More broadly, the ablation study shows that training loss alone is
insufficient for evaluating the network quality in this setting. Even
when the optimization appears successful in terms of aggregated loss
reduction, the predicted physical fields may still be inaccurate,
particularly for temperature, which is the stiffest component in the
present one-way coupled system. Therefore, field-wise validation against
a trusted numerical solver remains essential.

== Computational efficiency
<sec3_3>
In addition to predictive accuracy, a major motivation for the proposed
framework is to reduce the computational cost associated with repeated
evaluations over the design space. @tab:computational_cost
summarizes the wall-clock performance and approximate rental-cost
estimates of the reference FEM workflow and the proposed PINN surrogate,
and @fig:breakeven shows the corresponding cumulative wall-clock
break-even behavior.

The reference FEM simulations are carried out on a dual-socket Xeon 8570
node with 112 physical cores in total, with hyper-threading disabled and
all 16 memory channels populated. Each simulation uses 56 CPU cores and
requires 122.53~s per design point. By contrast, the parameterized PINN
is trained on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090D for 3~h 40~min, after which the
prediction for a new design requires only 9.3~ms. This corresponds to a
per-design speedup by a factor of approximately $1.32 times 10^4$,
indicating that the trained network can provide essentially real-time
thermohydraulic predictions across the design space.

Because the PINN involves a one-time training cost, its practical
benefit is best assessed in terms of the break-even point. Based on
wall-clock time, the training cost is amortized after approximately 108
design evaluations. Beyond this point, the cumulative time savings
increase nearly linearly with the number of additional queries. This
makes the proposed framework particularly attractive for applications
that require many repeated evaluations, such as design optimization,
sensitivity analysis, and interactive exploration of EMMC geometries.

A similar trend is observed when approximate hardware rental prices are
considered. Using representative market rates at the time of submission,
about 9 CNY/h for the CPU node and about 2 CNY/h for a single RTX 4090D
GPU, the monetary break-even point is reached after only about 24 design
evaluations. Nevertheless, wall-clock time is taken as the primary
metric in this work, since turnaround time is generally the more
relevant constraint in practical engineering design and model
development.

Although the FEM and PINN workflows are executed on different hardware
platforms, the comparison still reflects their realistic end-to-end
computational costs in practical use. In addition, the FEM reference is
evaluated in its efficient one-way coupled form, so the reported
acceleration is not attributable to an artificially weakened baseline.

= Conclusions
<sec4>
In this work, we develop a parameterized physics-informed neural network
for the efficient simulation of three-dimensional conjugate heat
transfer in embedded manifold microchannels for electronic cooling. The
proposed model accepts four geometric parameters together with spatial
coordinates as inputs, enabling continuous prediction across a design
space of practical interest. Through systematic validation against 625
finite-element simulations, the network achieves relative $L_2$ errors
of 8.1% and 5.6% for the velocity and temperature fields, respectively,
while reducing the per-design computational time and cost by over four
orders of magnitude.

The success of this framework rests on several methodological
innovations. A geometric ansatz embedding strategy explicitly encodes
the relative position of each point within the parameterized domain,
which is essential for training a single network across varying
geometries. A curriculum learning scheme that gradually increases the
effective Prandtl number and the fluid--solid conductivity ratio
stabilizes optimization in the convection-dominated regime. The use of
the SOAP optimizer combined with dynamic gradient normalization enables
robust convergence of this stiff multiphysics problem. Finally, a
JAX-based implementation with forward-mode automatic differentiation and
just-in-time compilation reduces the training time to less than four
hours on a consumer-grade GPU.

Ablation studies confirm that each of these components contributes
significantly to the final accuracy and convergence behavior. Notably,
the geometric ansatz is indispensable for parameterized geometries, and
curriculum learning helps steer the optimization toward the correct
solution branch even when the aggregated loss appears favorable. The
trained surrogate model reaches its break-even point after approximately
108 design evaluations in wall-clock time, making it highly attractive
for design optimization, sensitivity analysis, and interactive geometry
exploration.

Several directions remain for future work. First, extending the
parameterized formulation to a wider range of geometric features,
including manifold fin shapes and inlet/outlet configurations, would
further broaden the practical applicability. Second, incorporating
temperature-dependent material properties would address scenarios
involving large temperature rises or phase-change cooling. Third, while
the current model treats the flow as laminar and steady, extending the
framework to turbulent or transient regimes would be valuable for more
complex operating conditions. Finally, embedding the trained surrogate
within a gradient-based optimization loop could enable rapid, automated
design of next-generation embedded microfluidic coolers.

